1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
What if players could shape one living world from any browser,
while the browser itself did none of the heavy physics?

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:15,200
This is WorldWeaver: a multiplayer emergent world simulation
where players apply forces, and the world decides what happens next.

3
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,100
This is WorldWeaver. Rain creates water.
Water flows downhill and seeks its level.

4
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:26,800
Growth spreads vegetation where the conditions allow it.

5
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:31,400
Heat changes the environment and can ignite plants.

6
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:36,700
Every action costs influence, so players are shaping a shared ecosystem,
not simply painting a canvas.

7
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:43,200
The world is shared in real time.

8
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:48,000
A second player can join, appear in the player count,
and act on the same landscape.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,400
What one player changes, the other player sees immediately
because the server is authoritative.

10
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,700
The architecture is deliberately simple.

11
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:04,800
The Go server owns the simulation,
advances it at sixty ticks per second, and persists world state.

12
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:10,300
WebSockets broadcast updates.
Browsers handle input and rendering.

13
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:15,000
That makes the client thin, the state consistent,
and the experience accessible from a modern browser.

14
00:01:17,267 --> 00:01:22,267
Kiro was used throughout the entire project—from idea to shipment.

15
00:01:22,267 --> 00:01:27,267
Steering files defined the product vision and technical boundaries.

16
00:01:27,267 --> 00:01:33,267
Six specifications turned those boundaries into requirements,
designs, and implementation tasks.

17
00:01:33,267 --> 00:01:42,000
Hooks and tests kept everything verifiable as WorldWeaver grew
into a multiplayer simulation with physics, networking, persistence,
and a searchable element catalogue.

18
00:01:44,333 --> 00:01:49,000
WorldWeaver makes a computationally rich world feel lightweight and immediate.

19
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,133
One server, many players, and emergent consequences from every action.

20
00:01:53,133 --> 00:01:56,833
The world is expensive. The client is cheap.

21
00:01:56,833 --> 00:02:01,000
WorldWeaver: one world, many forces, built with Kiro.
